edit('06-scut3.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('In 2018 Gómez-Gálvez and colleagues found that cells lining curved living tissue take a shape nobody had named, the <i>scutoid</i>: five neighbours at the top, six at the bottom, and in between, two neighbours swap contact. It is the swap of this book seen inside a tissue: not two values changing places in an order, but two cells trading one neighbour for another as a slice moves through them.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW one swap section, figure-->', '''<h2 data-k="oneswap">One swap of neighbouring cells</h2>
<p>Take four cells round a short wall in a flat tissue: A and B share the wall, C sits above it and D below. Let A and B draw apart and C and D close in. The wall shrinks to a point where all four cells meet, and then reopens across: now C and D share a wall and A and B no longer touch (Figure {{f:oneswap}}). Foam physicists call it a T1 transition (Weaire and Rivier); biologists see it when tissue reshapes. Stack the frames in height and the cells become columns, and the two that met halfway up have gained a side: that column is a scutoid. One contact lost, one contact made, at one point: this is the swap that the rest of the book follows into four dimensions.</p>
''' + FIG('one-swap.png', 'oneswap', 'Three frames of a flat tissue of polygons: A and B touching, then all four cells A, B, C, D meeting at a point, then C and D touching', 'One swap of neighbouring cells in a flat tissue. Left: A and B (violet) share a wall. Middle: the wall has shrunk to a point where four cells meet. Right: C and D (red, the pair that swaps in) share a wall. Stacked in height, the cells that meet halfway gain a side: a scutoid.') +
 W('a scutoid', 'A cell with five neighbours on top and six below: halfway down one pair of its neighbours lets go and another pair meets, and the trade adds one triangle to the cell\'s side, the Y-corner of the next section. Counting faces: top pentagon and bottom hexagon, 5 side faces from above and one triangle, 1 + 1 + 5 + 1... the flat-faced scutoid of Theorem {{s:o5.2}}\'s tiling has 13 faces in all, every one flat.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW hand-off-->', HAND('A scutoid is a cell through which two neighbours swap contact. Layers of scutoids are slices of a solid one dimension up. What does the permutohedron of five values look like, and what does it do when a slice moves through it?'))])
# Chapter 7: move the Mirror-Law reading of the slices after the Mirror Law itself
