k = s.index('<h2 data-k="o10.6">'); s = s[:k] + block + s[k:]; open(p, 'w', encoding='utf8').write(s)
edit('07-web.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('Five values give 120 orders, the corners of a solid in four dimensions. Joined by swaps they make a web with four swaps at every knot; its squares and hexagons are the rules the swaps obey. Cut through its centre, the solid shows a Vacuus of 65 slices, and at its heart five of DICTO\'s 1:2 Truncated Octahedra slot together. Which faces lie on mirrors is told by one law, read off a chain of numbers.')),
 ('after', '<h2 data-k="o6.2">The 4D Vacuus</h2>', ''),
 ('before', '<h2 data-k="o6.2">The 4D Vacuus</h2>', '''<p><b>The rules among swaps.</b> Two swaps can meet on the solid in only two ways (Corollary {{s:o1.2}}). If they move values far apart in size, such as 1, 2 and 4, 5, they commute: either order gives the same result, and the four orders form a square. If they share a value, such as 1, 2 and 2, 3, they braid: three swaps each way round give the same result, and the six orders form a hexagon (Figure {{f:rules}}). The 90 squares and 60 hexagons of the 4D permutohedron are all the rules there are: the classical presentation of the group of orders by neighbour swaps (Coxeter; Humphreys), drawn as faces.</p>
''' + FIG('square-hexagon.png', 'rules', 'Left: a square whose corners are four orders of five values, two sides red for the swap 1 and 2, two black for 4 and 5. Right: a hexagon of six orders whose sides alternate the swaps 1 and 2 (red) and 2 and 3', 'The two rules among swaps, as 2-faces of the 4D permutohedron. Left: swaps of far-apart values commute (a square). Right: swaps sharing a value braid, s₁s₂s₁ = s₂s₁s₂ (a hexagon). The swap 1↔2 is red in both.')),
 ('before', '<h2 data-k="o6.3">', FIG('four-slices.png', 'fourslices', 'Four three-dimensional solids: the slices of the four kinds, each with the faces of the 4D permutohedron that lie in it gold', 'The four kinds of central slice of Theorem {{s:o6.1}}, recomputed from their normals (8, −7, −7, 3, 3), (6, −9, 1, 1, 1), (3, −2, −2, −2, 3) and (1, 1, −4, 1, 1). Gold: the 2-faces of the 4D permutohedron that lie in the slice, two squares, two hexagons, two hexagons and six squares; pale: where the slice crosses cells. The fourth is the DICTO 1:2, five of which make the heart.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW mirror law intro-->', '''<h2 data-k="mirrorintro">The Queens' Vacuus and the Mirror Law</h2>
<p>The heart of the 4D Vacuus is the permutohedron's own: none of its slices is a mirror. The rest of this chapter retells #107, which asks the same question of every polytope built from a reflection group, the Queens among them, and answers it by one rule read off the group's chain. The rule decides which faces lie on mirrors, the fixed places of the group's reflections; Book Eight, <i>The Platonic Solids</i>, applies it to the five solids (its Chapter 4, a retelling), and this chapter is its home.</p>
'''),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW #91, worked example, hand-off-->', '''<p><b>Permutohedra inside the Queens' cousins.</b> The permutohedra of four values appear inside other four-dimensional solids too. In the tesseract trimmed by its polar 16-cell, K ∩ μK*, at μ = φ², 8 of the 24 cells are Icosaxes, the permutohedron of four values at the golden stop of Chapter {{ch:slider}}, and the other 16 are Lesser Golden Cut Tetrahedra: every cell is a golden cut (#91; home Book Nine, <i>The Golden Ladder</i>, Chapter 8).</p>
''' + W('the 150 faces on 65 slices', 'The 2-faces of the 4D permutohedron are the splits of five places into three blocks of consecutive values. Blocks of sizes 3, 1, 1 in some order give the hexagons: 5!/(3! 1! 1!) = 20 for each of the three orders of the sizes, 60 in all. Blocks 2, 2, 1 give the squares: 5!/(2! 2! 1!) = 30 for each of three orders, 90 in all. 60 + 90 = 150. Each lies on one central slice, and the slices number 20 + 30 + 10 + 5 = 65 (Theorem {{s:o6.1}}): a split and its reverse share a slice, and the five heart slices carry six faces each.') +
 HAND('The 4D permutohedron is the web of swaps: its squares say that far-apart swaps commute, its hexagons that three values braid, and its Vacuus carries all of them on 65 central slices, five DICTO 1:2 at its heart. Now sweep it.'))])
edit('08-centre1.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener, warm-up-->', OPEN('Pass a flat sheet up through a solid and watch the slice. In three dimensions the slice is a polygon that gains and loses sides. In four dimensions the slice is a whole solid whose faces are pieces of the cells, a tissue, and as the sheet rises cells are born, swap neighbours and die, like the cells of a growing organ. This is DICTO\'s scutoid tissue, the centre of the book: the solid of all orders of five values, swept, is a tissue of 26 births, 66 swaps and 26 deaths.') + '''<h2 data-k="warmup">A warm-up in three dimensions: Kelvin's cell swept</h2>
<p>Take Kelvin's cell, the 24 orders of 1, 2, 3, 4, and a height u · x with u<sub>1</sub> &lt; u<sub>2</sub> &lt; u<sub>3</sub> &lt; u<sub>4</sub>. Each corner has three edges, and an edge goes down when it swaps a value k standing to the left of k + 1 (the argument of Proposition {{s:o5.1}} below). So the corners with 0, 1, 2, 3 edges below number 1, 11, 11, 1, the Eulerian numbers of four values. A slice is a polygon whose sides are cut from the faces of the solid. At a corner with one edge below the polygon gains a side and two of its sides stop meeting; at a corner with two below it loses a side and two sides begin to meet (Figure {{f:kswept}}). In three dimensions the gain and the loss of a contact come at different corners; one dimension up they come together.</p>
''' + FIG('kelvin-swept.png', 'kswept', 'Five copies of Kelvin\'s cell in pale glass, each cut by a rising plane whose section polygon is red; the corners already passed are marked by the number of their edges below', 'Kelvin\'s cell swept in five frames. The section (red outline) is a polygon; the corners already passed are dotted by their edges below: 0 grey, 1 gold, 2 red, 3 violet, in all 1, 11, 11, 1.')),
 ('rep', '<h2 data-k="o7.1">The boundary of a simple 4-polytope is a scutoid tissue</h2>', '<h2 data-k="o7.1">The 4D permutohedron swept: births, swaps and deaths</h2>'),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW prior-art sentence-->', '<p><b>What is new.</b> In the sources checked (listed in #104: MathWorld and Wolfram on the scutoid, the Buchstaber invariant of simple polytopes, The Best Ways to Slice a Polytope, Izmestiev, Klee and Novik on simplicial moves of balanced complexes, and web searches of 10 and 11 November 2026 for scutoids in four and higher dimensions and for sweeps of simple polytopes by bistellar flips), no earlier work reads the sweep of a polytope as a scutoid tissue or links scutoids to the h-vector. The counting of corners by the h-vector is classical (McMullen; Bruggesser and Mani); the reading as births, swaps and deaths is DICTO\'s.</p>\n'),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW self-reverse, figures, hand-off-->', FIG('film-strip-4d.png', 'filmstrip', 'Nine three-dimensional slices of the 4D permutohedron, small at the ends and largest in the middle, each tiled by the sections of its cells; one cell gold, two red', 'The 4D permutohedron swept: nine slices from bottom to top, each a 3D solid tiled by the sections of the cells it crosses (the tissue). Gold: one cell, a permutohedron of four values; red: the pair of cells at the nearest swap, still touching just before it or just met after it. Above each frame: the cells in the slice and the births, swaps and deaths passed so far.') +
 FIG('eulerian-bars.png', 'eulerbars', 'Five bars of heights 1, 26, 66, 26, 1 labelled first corner, births, swaps, deaths, last corner', 'The sweep of the 4D permutohedron counted: corners with 0 to 4 edges below, 1, 26, 66, 26, 1, the Eulerian numbers of five values and its h-vector. The swaps, red, are the middle.', 'max-width:70%;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto') +
 W('the 66 swaps', 'A corner is an order of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Read it left to right and count the values k that stand somewhere to the left of k + 1. In 1 2 3 4 5 all four do: the top corner. In 5 4 3 2 1 none do: the bottom corner. In 2 1 4 3 5 two do (2 before 3, and 4 before 5... and 1 before 2? No: 1 stands after 2), so it has two edges below and is a swap. The orders with exactly two such k number 66, with one 26, with three 26: the Eulerian numbers 1, 26, 66, 26, 1.') +
 '''<h2 data-k="selfreverse">The swap that reverses to itself</h2>
<p>Run the sweep backwards, by the height −u. Every edge that went down now goes up, so a corner with i edges below becomes one with 4 − i below: every birth becomes a death and every death a birth. A swap stays a swap: the pair of cells that let go now meets, and the pair that met now lets go. Among the moves of a four-dimensional tissue the scutoid swap is the one event that is its own reverse, and Theorem {{s:o7.1}} counts it by h<sub>2</sub>, the middle of the h-vector, in every direction. This is a law at the fixed point of a swap, the reversal of the sweep, and it holds for a stated reason: the reversal fixes only the middle index (studies/2026-11-13-swaps-and-fixed-points, row 4, checked exactly for d = 2 to 7).</p>
<p>The self-reverse class exists only in even dimension, where the middle index i = d/2 is a whole number; and only in dimension four is it the pure swap, one contact lost and one made at a single corner. In three dimensions the contact lost and the contact gained come at different corners (the warm-up above); in five they come at the two indices 2 and 3, which are each other's reverse; in six and above the middle corners touch only lower faces (Theorem {{s:o7.1}}′ in Chapter {{ch:centre2}}). The scutoid swap of a tissue is a four-dimensional event.</p>
''' + HAND('The 30 cells of the 4D permutohedron, sliced, are a tissue: 26 births, 66 swaps, 26 deaths, the swaps the one move that is its own reverse. Is this special to the permutohedron?'))])
edit('09-centre2.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('The permutohedron is not special. DICTO found that every solid in four dimensions with four cells at each corner, swept by a moving slice, is a tissue of births, swaps and deaths, and that the numbers are the solid\'s h-vector, the same in every direction. Between the two Queens, the 600-cell and the 120-cell, the swaps balance exactly: as many trade a contact of one kind for the other as the other way round, 601 each way.') + '<h2 data-k="everysimple">Every simple 4-polytope</h2>\n<p>Theorem {{s:o7.1}} of Chapter {{ch:centre1}} was stated for every simple 4-polytope, and its proof used nothing of the permutohedron but its four cells at a corner. This chapter carries it through every dimension and along the road between the Queens.</p>\n'),
 ('after', '<p><b>Interpretation.</b> The Queens are the two ends of a channel and this layer, where their cells meet, is where they communicate, equally in both directions.</p>', '\n' + FIG('bt600-balance.png', 'btbalance', 'Three step curves rising with the height of a sweep: red and violet both end at 601, a grey one at 964', 'The balance law at work: one generic sweep of the bitruncated 600-cell, recomputed from its 3600 corners and 720 cells. Red: the swaps that trade a contact between truncated tetrahedra (W–W) for one between truncated icosahedra (R–R); violet: the reverse; grey: the mixed swaps. Red and violet climb on different schedules and finish level, 601 each; 601 + 601 + 964 = 2166 = h<sub>2</sub>.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW worked example, figures, hand-off-->', W('601 each way', 'The bitruncated 600-cell has 600 truncated tetrahedra (W) and 120 truncated icosahedra (R), two of each at every corner. Its R cells meet across 720 faces (the decagon-free faces between truncated icosahedra, one for each edge of the 600-cell\'s polar... in short, one R–R face for each of the 720 pentagons of the 120-cell). Every R–R contact appears once in a sweep: at the first corner (one), at the birth of an R cell (118 of them, all R cells but the two at the first corner), or by a W–W → R–R swap. So these swaps number 720 − 118 − 1 = 601, and by the deaths the same number go the other way.') +
 FIG('h-vectors.png', 'hvectors', 'Left: grouped bars of the h-vectors of four simple 4-polytopes on a log scale. Right: four bars 6, 17, 73, 601', 'Left: the h-vectors (births h₁, swaps h₂, deaths h₃) of the 120-cell, the truncated 120-cell, the bitruncated 600-cell and the 4D permutohedron, each from its numbers of corners and cells by the Dehn–Sommerville relations. Right: the balanced swaps at the middle of each chain, E − V + 1 of its regular polytope: 6, 17, 73, 601.') +
 HAND('Every simple 4-polytope sweeps as a scutoid tissue counted by its h-vector, and between the Queens its swaps balance. In a simple polytope the swaps come one at a time. What happens when many crowd into one corner?'))])
